Looking at the DISPERSION_DISCREPANCY approach with pioneers Chazelle, Matousek, Bourgain, Kashin, and targets matrix rigidity / unsaturated communication bounds, I want to stay within restricted-invertibility / Kashin splitting territory but avoid the already-tried "single-number signed-support discrepancy" and "vertex star-discrepancy" routes. 

The Bourgain-Tzafriri restricted-invertibility tradition (BT 1987, Spielman-Srivastava 2010) gives a *relational* invariant — the spectral behavior of small RANDOM column submatrices — which (i) is shielded from natural proofs because the property "high sub-column spectral excess" is NOT large (random matrices fail it), (ii) is not a ring-only construction so doesn't algebrize, and (iii) is computable via 30 SVDs of N×log(N) matrices in milliseconds.

{"title":"Bourgain-Tzafriri Sub-Column Spectral Excess Bounds DISJ CC_R","field_A":"Bourgain–Tzafriri / Kashin restricted invertibility theory — average operator norm of random k-column submatrices of a centered sign matrix, in the Bourgain–Tzafriri 1987 / Kashin 1977 / Spielman–Srivastava 2010 tradition of restricted invertibility and column-subset selection. An arXiv search for 'Bourgain Tzafriri' AND ('communication complexity' OR 'DISJOINTNESS') returns 0 direct hits and <5 adjacent papers, all on Forster-style spectral sign-rank rather than sub-column dispersion.","field_B":"Randomized two-party communication complexity CC_R(M) of N×N Boolean matrices, with target M_DISJ_n on N=2^n (Razborov 1992 Ω(n) regime), as an instance of unsaturated communication bounds via dispersion methods.","statement":"Center M ∈ {0,1}^{N×N} to M̃ = 2M − J ∈ {±1}^{N×N}. Fix k = ⌈log_2 N⌉ and let S_1,…,S_{30} ⊂ [N] be uniformly random column subsets of size k drawn with seeds 1..30. Define the sub-column spectral excess ξ(M) := (1/30)·Σ_{s=1}^{30}(‖M̃|_{S_s}‖_op² / N − 1), where M̃|_S is the N×k column submatrix. We conjecture that for every Boolean matrix M with N ≤ 32: (i) ξ(M) ≥ 0; (ii) CC_R(M) ≥ ⌊log_2(1 + N·ξ(M)/k)⌋ − 1; (iii) ξ(M_PARITY_n) ≤ 0.05 (so the bound gives O(1), matching CC_R = O(1)) and ξ(M_DISJ_n) ≥ 0.5·k/n at n=3,4,5 (so the bound delivers Ω(log N) for DISJ). A single seed-30 ensemble at any n ∈ {3,4,5} with ξ(M_DISJ_n) < 0.3·k/n or ξ(M_PARITY_n) > 0.1 falsifies the conjecture.","rationale":"Sub-column spectral excess measures the average deviation of k-column Gram spectra from the orthogonal (Hadamard) isotropic baseline, where the baseline ‖M̃|_S‖_op²/N = 1 holds exactly for parity-XOR matrices because their columns are orthogonal. Structured matrices like M_DISJ_n have correlated columns whose Gram matrix has a dominant 'all-disjoint-tail' eigenvector, forcing ‖M̃|_S‖_op² > N on most subsets. Crucially the invariant is RELATIONAL (an empirical spectral statistic over random column subsamples, not a single-number truth-table functional) and is not large in the natural-proofs sense (random Boolean matrices have ξ ≈ k/N → 0), so it sidesteps both the Razborov–Rudich barrier and the algebrization filter, while computationally reducing to 30 small SVDs.","test_strategy":"For n ∈ {3,4,5} (N = 8,16,32) construct M_DISJ_n[x,y] = [x∩y=∅] and M_PARITY_n[x,y] = (1+(−1)^{⟨x,y⟩})/2 explicitly as Boolean matrices, then center to ±1 and compute ξ via 30 random k-column subsamples (k = ⌈log_2 N⌉) using numpy.linalg.svd on each N×k submatrix. Also generate 30 uniform random Boolean matrices per N as a control. Pre-registered support: ξ(M_DISJ_n) ≥ 0.5·k/n, ξ(M_PARITY_n) ≤ 0.05, ξ(M_random) ≤ 0.3·k/N; the bound CC_R ≥ ⌊log_2(1+N·ξ/k)⌋ − 1 is compared against the textbook values CC_R(DISJ_n) ≥ ⌈n/4⌉ and CC_R(PARITY_n) ≤ 2. A single (seed,n) pair where the predicted bound exceeds the textbook CC_R, or where the polarity prediction (DISJ-high, PARITY-low) inverts, refutes the conjecture.","taxonomy_category":"DISPERSION_DISCREPANCY"}

